WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.361 --> 00:00:02.783
Here is the practical test: can an

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:05.667
AI agent move from a prompt in

3
00:00:05.728 --> 00:00:08.571
OpenCode to a circuit whose performance we

4
00:00:08.571 --> 00:00:09.552
can verify?

5
00:00:10.414 --> 00:00:12.616
Start with a clear installation task.

6
00:00:13.016 --> 00:00:15.599
Detect the operating system, find the
existing

7
00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:17.541
tools, and check their connections.

8
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:21.005
In this recording, two Ubuntu environments
caused

9
00:00:21.005 --> 00:00:24.208
confusion. The useful result is an
identified

10
00:00:24.208 --> 00:00:27.332
working environment, with versions and
connection tests

11
00:00:27.332 --> 00:00:28.073
documented.

12
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:31.554
Next, ask for a small test circuit.

13
00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:33.835
Tool discovery is only one check.

14
00:00:34.136 --> 00:00:36.576
The agent should create a schematic, run

15
00:00:36.597 --> 00:00:39.658
electrical rules checking, export its
connections, and

16
00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:41.739
test the result with ngspice.

17
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:44.320
Keep the reports, including any fixes.

18
00:00:45.721 --> 00:00:47.903
Now make the engineering target explicit.

19
00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:50.205
We need a voltage gain between nine

20
00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:52.087
point five and ten point five, and

21
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:54.490
a minus three decibel bandwidth of at

22
00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.371
least one hundred and fifty kilohertz.

23
00:00:57.072 --> 00:00:59.855
The specified op-amp model has an
open-loop

24
00:00:59.855 --> 00:01:01.877
gain of one hundred thousand and a

25
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:03.799
one megahertz gain-bandwidth product.

26
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.704
A single non-inverting stage reaches a
gain

27
00:01:07.704 --> 00:01:10.508
of about ten, but its simulated bandwidth

28
00:01:10.568 --> 00:01:12.370
is only about one hundred kilohertz.

29
00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:13.832
It misses the target.

30
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:16.135
This is why the prompt should require

31
00:01:16.135 --> 00:01:18.879
feasibility analysis before a pass
declaration.

32
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.185
The recorded design uses two cascaded
stages.

33
00:01:23.385 --> 00:01:26.069
Each has a twenty-two kilohm feedback
resistor

34
00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:28.232
and a ten kilohm resistor to ground.

35
00:01:28.592 --> 00:01:30.395
The op-amp model stays the same.

36
00:01:30.515 --> 00:01:32.618
We change the circuit architecture, then
rerun

37
00:01:32.618 --> 00:01:33.139
the checks.

38
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.801
For this edit, I reran the original

39
00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:39.522
circuit files. The fresh simulation gives
a

40
00:01:39.522 --> 00:01:41.462
gain of about ten point two four

41
00:01:41.823 --> 00:01:43.663
and a bandwidth of about two hundred

42
00:01:43.663 --> 00:01:44.664
and one kilohertz.

43
00:01:45.044 --> 00:01:46.304
Both meet the targets.

44
00:01:46.604 --> 00:01:49.285
The KiCad netlist matches the original
circuit,

45
00:01:49.645 --> 00:01:52.586
and electrical rules checking reports zero
violations.

46
00:01:53.927 --> 00:01:57.009
These are small-signal results from a
simplified,

47
00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:00.091
single-pole model. They do not establish
real

48
00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:03.133
hardware performance, output limits, or
noise.

49
00:02:03.913 --> 00:02:08.016
The evidence chain matters: editable
schematic, exported

50
00:02:08.016 --> 00:02:11.898
netlist, documented model, solver data,
and a

51
00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.979
comparison with the original requirements.

52
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.383
A professional engineering prompt defines
the task,

53
00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.846
the checks, and the evidence required for

54
00:02:20.846 --> 00:02:23.929
success. Let the agent coordinate the
work.

55
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.972
Let verified results determine the
conclusion.
